# --- Python / env -----------------------------------------------------------
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.DS_Store

# --- secrets ----------------------------------------------------------------
.env

# --- per-operator runtime state ---------------------------------------------
# runs/ is this operator's campaign: queue, generated variants, health and gate
# snapshots, logs, work directories. That IS machine- and host-specific and is not the
# shared artifact.
#
# RESULT RECORDS ARE THE EXCEPTION, and the exception matters. papers/ makes claims whose
# every number tools/coe.py audits against a registry built ONLY from
# runs/sweep/results/*.json. With those ignored, a fresh clone cannot rebuild the registry
# and cannot run the audit at all: E1 reports 706 problems, E5 finds all 117 paper numbers
# uncheckable, and the chain of evidence -- the thing this project is chiefly about -- is
# intact on one laptop and broken for every reader. A provenance system whose provenance
# does not ship is a provenance system in name only.
#
# 552KB of JSON is a small price for a paper a stranger can check. The records are append-
# only and were never edited, so committing them is publishing evidence, not state.
# git cannot re-include a file inside an EXCLUDED DIRECTORY, so `runs/` followed by
# `!runs/sweep/results/` silently did nothing: the negations never applied and the result
# records were tracked only because they had been force-added once. Future records would
# have stopped being committed with no error, quietly undoing the provenance fix. Each
# level must be unignored in turn.
runs/*
!runs/sweep/
runs/sweep/*
!runs/sweep/results/
!runs/sweep/decisions/
# The queue defines which experiments exist and the tests read it; without it a clone
# cannot run the suite. Small, and it is the campaign's plan rather than host state.
!runs/sweep/queue.json
# Decision records are evidence too: they are what lets a reader ask whether the campaign
# would choose the same experiment again from the same state, which is the question two
# external reviews said this project could not answer.
!runs/sweep/decisions/
tmp/

# --- literature corpus ------------------------------------------------------
# Fetched full texts are large, re-fetchable with `tools/lit.py fetch`, and are
# third-party content. The corpus INDEX is derived and per-operator. What IS committed
# is the knowledge extracted from them -- claims, mechanisms, hypotheses, lessons --
# because that is the part a future run needs and cannot regenerate.
lit/sources/
# lit/index.json carries each source's sha256 and fetch status. E1 needs it to tell an
# absent-but-indexed source from one that was never recorded, so without it a clone
# cannot distinguish 'not fetched here' from 'no provenance at all'. It is small.
!lit/index.json
lit/ACTIVE_DIRECTION.json

# --- profiling captures -----------------------------------------------------
*.nsys-rep
*.qdstrm

# --- remote host config (address, login, port) ---
.ophis_host

# Sharded screen scratch, merged into lit/index.json. Per-operator and re-derivable.
lit/index_shard*.json
lit/*_progress.json
